      "id": "timer-02",
      "verdict": "contradicted",
      "line": 73,
      "fragment": "Stop does not wait for f to complete before returning.",
      "reason": "Once the AfterFunc callback has started, Stop can return while it is still running. The caller must coordinate with the callback to observe completion.",
      "question": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "timer-03",
      "verdict": "supported",
      "line": 123,
      "fragment": "to run again, in which case it returns false.",
      "reason": "After the callback starts, its timer has expired. Reset on that expired timer returns false and schedules another execution. A further Reset while the timer is active reschedules the pending execution and returns true.",
      "question": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "timer-04",
      "verdict": "contradicted",
      "line": 126,
      "fragment": "goroutine running f does not run concurrently with the prior",
      "reason": "When Reset returns false and schedules another execution, the new goroutine may run while the prior callback is still running.",
      "question": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "timer-05",
      "verdict": "supported",
      "line": 67,
      "fragment": "It returns true if the call stops the timer",
      "reason": "Stop can cancel a pending execution on an active AfterFunc timer before it expires and returns true when it does. Another Stop returns false while the timer remains stopped. A subsequent Reset can schedule a new execution.",
      "question": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "timer-06",
      "verdict": "contradicted",
      "line": 178,
      "fragment": "newTimer(when(d), 0, goFunc, f, nil)",
      "reason": "AfterFunc passes the function value supplied at registration as the timer argument. At expiration, goFunc invokes that argument, so rebinding only the caller's variable leaves the registered function value intact.",
      "question": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "timer-07",
      "verdict": "unspecified",
      "line": 173,
      "fragment": "AfterFunc waits for the duration to elapse and then calls f",
      "reason": "The component defines when it invokes the registered callback. It provides no application retry policy.",
      "question": "OPEN: Must the application retry a timed-out operation exactly three times?"
    }
